{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_ccb6f1e9a6d4648b8979929420c131251147ae5e8306179d0893fcb0db3ff484", "title": "Telegraph City, California", "text": "Telegraph City is an unincorporated community in Calaveras County, California. It lies at an elevation of 653 feet (199 m). A post office operated here from 1862 to 1894. The place was named for its location on the telegraph line between Stockton and Sonora.", "score": 23.9372}, {"id": "hp_doc_3b347830ad3d9e423e71373e6d4f03cee3759dd1dee3e0c1b998ab1dce1492b1", "title": "Deseret Telegraph Company", "text": "The Deseret Telegraph Company ( ) was a telegraphy company headquartered in Salt Lake City, Utah, United States. The company was organized in 1867 to direct operation of the recently completed Deseret Telegraph Line; its largest stakeholder was The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. The Deseret line ran north and south through the Utah Territory, connecting the numerous settlements with Salt Lake City and the First Transcontinental Telegraph. The company was dissolved in 1900 when its assets, including the Deseret line, were sold to the Western Union Telegraph Company.", "score": 14.285}, {"id": "hp_doc_923eb7ccc712c02e67c5b11678373718f9c39af7f6aaa270e3ad8056bd99a135", "title": "Moreton Telegraph Station", "text": "The Moreton Telegraph Office, located on the banks of the Wenlock River was part of the Cape York Telegraph Line and was completed in 1887.", "score": 11.4066}], "remaining_searches": 2}
